  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonable warmth and dry weather continue \nWednesday\n\nMid-level ridging and surface high pressure continue to dominate the \npattern through early Thursday. A thermal ridge with 925mb \ntemperatures +14 to +17C positions across southern New England.\nMainly sunny skies, fairly dry soils, and SW flow will typically \nsupport better mixing than most guidance anticipates. This will \nlikely favor temperatures warmer than what model guidance projects. \nHigh temperatures Wednesday are likely to be in the upper 60s to low \n70s range, with mid 70s possible in the valleys. Can't rule out \ntemperatures overperforming reaching the upper 70s. NBM \nprobabilities show low to moderate probabilities (20-50%) for \ngreater than 75 degrees in portions of the CT Valley and Boston \nMetro. If this does happen, records have potential to be met or \nbroken. Record at BOS is 78, PVD 77, ORH 74, BDL 78. \n\nA warm day will be followed by a warm night. Overnight temperatures \nfall into the 50s which is about average for our high temperatures \nthis time of year. Some locations may have a shot at \nmeeting/breaking the high min record. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Wet and unsettled weather returns Thursday \nthrough the weekend with continued warmer temperatures to start \nbefore cooler temps arrive Sunday and beyond.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to herald the end of our highly \nanomalous stretch of dry weather by Thursday, kicking off a period \nof 36-48 hours of periodic rainfall, sometimes heavy. This comes \ncourtesy of a few things; first is an approaching front extending \nfrom a trough of low pressure crossing the Great Lakes. After moving \nthrough NY the front stalls draped across New England from SW to NE \nlate Thursday. This brings the initial round of rain chances for \nThursday, mainly for western areas of SNE while much of the region \nsoutheast of a BDL-ORH line may stay dry. A better shot of \nwidespread and heavier rainfall then arrives for Friday into early \nSaturday as a secondary wave of low pressure forms along the front. \nIt deepens as it lifts north in the vicinity of Cape Cod Saturday \nand then even moreso over the Gulf of Maine on Sunday by which time \nwe'll be on the drier side. \n\nThere will be a lot of moisture for this system to work with as \npersistent moist SW flow ahead of the upper trough pulls in PWATs \nover 1.5\" and dewpoints into the low 60s! This will lead to very \nwarm low temps Friday morning (in the low 60s) but more importantly, \nfuel for the good dynamics to work with. Those elevated dewpoints \nwill make for some instability allowing for some convection to \ncontribute to localized downpours, while convergence around the low \nand placement beneath the RRQ of a 105kt upper jet will provide more \nlift in the meso/synoptic scale. Exact placement of the swath of \nheaviest rainfall will unfortunately not be nailed down until we get \ncloser to the event as it will be determined by where exactly the \nfront stalls. Generally ensemble guidance is calling for a 1-3\" \nrainfall with EPS and GEFS calling for a 50-60% and 30-40% chance of \nAOA 1\" of rainfall respectively, centered over central and eastern \nSNE. Fortunately, we've been very dry and Flash Flood Guidance is \nhigh. Thus, not expecting flash flooding to be an issue though some \npoor drainage/urban flooding is possible especially given the leaves \nblocking drainage this time of year. \n\nBeyond Saturday we'll see some scattered showers continuing as the \nupper trough is slow to exit overhead; nothing like Friday/Saturday, \nthough, as the atmospheric column begins to dry out going into next \nweek. Behind the trough colder NW flow ushers in a colder (closer to \nnormal) airmass for early next week as high temperatures return to \nthe 50s.",
